"""CS331 Chapter 1 Part 2 (protocol layering, OSI, TCP/IP): explaining notes and explained questions, by slide.

NOTES replace a slide's plain notes in order (highlights link to note numbers); extra notes go at the end.
Q replaces the deck's old questions. No tutorial question comes from this deck, so all are predictions.
"""
from .common import pick, truth, written

NOTES = {
    1: ['The whole deck answers one question: how do you organise something as complicated as a network? The answer is **layers**. You meet the idea first, then the OSI model (7 layers), then how data is wrapped and unwrapped as it moves through them (**encapsulation**), then what each layer does, which unit of data (PDU) it handles and which address it uses, and finally the TCP/IP model (5 layers) that the Internet actually runs.',
        'The table on slide 51 (layer, job, PDU, address) is the most reused thing in the course. Chapter 2 is the physical layer, Chapters 3 to 6 are the data link layer, Chapters 7 and 8 are the network layer, Chapter 9 is transport and Chapter 10 is application. If you know the table, you always know where a new topic sits.'],
    2: ['Three sections: **1.4 protocol layering** (why layers, how layers talk), **1.5 the TCP/IP suite** and **1.6 the OSI model**. The slides actually teach OSI first and then show how TCP/IP maps onto it, so read them in that order.'],
    3: ['A network involves cables, signals, addresses, routes, error checks, applications and much more. If one program had to handle all of it, nobody could build it, understand it or fix it. The question on the slide is how developers and users can cope with that complexity.',
        'The solution is to split the work into **layers**. A layer is a **logical grouping** of related functions: one layer only worries about turning bits into signals, another only about finding a route, another only about getting a whole message to the right program. Each layer solves one part of the problem and trusts the others to do theirs, and the layers exist on every node in the network.',
        'Layers are stacked, and each one **uses the services of the layer below** and **provides a service to the layer above**: layer n-1 serves layer n. The meeting point between two neighbouring layers is called the **interface**. It defines exactly what information, operations and services the lower layer offers the upper one, like a menu. As long as the interface stays the same, a layer’s inside can be changed without affecting anyone else.'],
    4: ['Read the figure in two directions. **Horizontally**, layer 3 on host 1 follows the same rules as layer 3 on host 2: those rules are the **layer-3 protocol**, and the two layer-3s are called **peers**. **Vertically**, each layer hands data to the layer directly below it on the same host through an **interface**. So: protocol = between the same layer on different machines; interface = between adjacent layers on the same machine.',
        'The peers never actually send anything directly to each other. Layer 3 on host 1 passes its data down to layer 2, then to layer 1, and only **layer 1 puts signals on the physical medium**. On host 2 the data goes back up. The horizontal conversation is **logical** (it feels direct to the layer), while the real path is down, across and up.'],
    5: ['Not every device on the path needs every layer. The **hosts** (source A and destination B) use **all five**, because the data starts and ends in an application. A **link-layer switch** only uses **layers 1 and 2**: it reads the frame’s MAC address and forwards the frame on the same network. A **router** uses **layers 1 to 3**: it reads the IP address to decide which network to send the packet to next. Neither looks at the transport or application data.',
         'The more layers a device implements, the more work and processing it does, so the more complex it is. A host is the most complex, a router is in the middle, a switch is simpler, and a repeater or hub (layer 1 only) is the simplest. This ranking comes back in Chapter 6 on connecting devices.'],
    6: ['The first advantage is **simplicity**: one huge, complicated process becomes several smaller, simpler ones, so the hardware and software for each layer are **easier to implement** and easier to test and fix.',
        'The second advantage comes from two rules: each layer has its **own distinct set of protocols** (no two layers do the same job), and there is a **well-defined interface** between neighbouring layers. Together these mean each layer can be **built independently**. A network card maker can build layers 1 and 2, an operating system maker layers 3 and 4, a browser maker layer 7, and the result still works because each respects the interfaces. That lets **different manufacturers** work on different layers, which encourages both collaboration and competition.'],
    7: ['The set of protocols used by all the layers together is called a **protocol stack**, because the protocols are stacked one above the other, like the layers.',
        'There are two standard stacks. **OSI** (Open Systems Interconnection) is the 7-layer reference model from ISO; it is mainly used to teach and discuss networking. **TCP/IP** is the suite the Internet actually runs. You need both: OSI for the vocabulary (people still say “layer 7” or “a layer-3 switch”), and TCP/IP for what really happens.'],
    8: ['**OSI** stands for Open Systems Interconnection. It was developed by **ISO**, the International Organization for Standardization, in the 1970s, as a framework for standardising networks. An **open system** is a set of protocols that lets any two systems communicate **regardless of their underlying architecture**: an IBM machine and a Mac can talk as long as both follow the model. The model describes how data moves from an application on one computer to an application on another. It dominated before 1990, after which TCP/IP took over in practice. Watch the letters: **ISO** is the organisation, **OSI** is the model.'],
    9: ['Number the layers from the bottom: **1 Physical**, **2 Data link**, **3 Network**, **4 Transport**, **5 Session**, **6 Presentation**, **7 Application**. Exams ask both ways: “which layer is number 4?” (transport) and “which layer is above the network layer?” (transport). Being able to count up and down the stack quickly saves time on every later chapter.'],
    10: ['The slide’s sentence goes **top-down**: **A**ll **P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing gives Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data link, Physical. If you prefer counting from layer 1 upwards, use the bottom-up sentence in the tip below.'],
    11: ['A layer can be built in **software**, in **hardware**, or both. The upper layers deal with data and programs, so they are software; the bottom layers deal with signals and timing, so they need hardware. The split: **application, presentation, session and transport** are **software** (part of the operating system and the applications). The **network** layer is **mixed** hardware and software (routers have special forwarding hardware, and hosts run IP in software). The **data link and physical** layers are in **hardware**, on the **network interface card (NIC)**: the card builds the frames, checks the MAC address and turns bits into signals.'],
    12: ['**Peers** are the processes at the **same layer** on different machines; the communication between them is called **peer-to-peer**. In the figure, device A’s layer 4 is the peer of device B’s layer 4, even though their data actually travels down, across and up.',
         'Look at which layers the intermediate nodes have: only **1 to 3**. That means layers **4 to 7 talk end to end**: A’s transport layer talks directly to B’s, and the routers in between never touch it. Layers **1 to 3 talk hop by hop**: A talks to the first router, which talks to the next, and so on. This end-to-end vs. hop-by-hop split is one of the most tested ideas in the deck.'],
    13: ['Each layer talks to its peer using **formatted blocks of data** called **Protocol Data Units (PDUs)**. A PDU has two parts: the **data (payload)** handed down by the layer above, and the current layer’s own **control information** (requests and instructions for its peer). Every layer builds its own PDU, which is why each layer’s PDU has a different name (slide 51).',
         'The control information goes in a **header** in front of the data. The **data link layer is the only exception**: it adds a header **and a trailer** at the end. The trailer holds the error-detection code (such as a CRC), which is placed at the end so it can be calculated as the bits go out.',
         'A header is read and acted on **only by the peer layer on the receiving node**. The receiver’s transport layer reads the transport header; it never looks at the network header (the network layer already removed it). That rule is what keeps the layers independent.'],
    14: ['Follow the data down the left side: the application’s data D7 gets header **H7**, then the presentation layer adds **H6** to that whole block, and so on down to the data link layer, which adds **H2** and the trailer **T2**. The physical layer sends the result as a stream of bits. On the right side the reverse happens: each layer removes its own header and passes the rest up, until the application gets D7 back. Note that each lower layer treats everything from above (headers included) as plain data.'],
    15: ['**Encapsulation** means a layer puts the entire PDU from the layer above inside its own data field, **without looking inside or changing it**. The network layer doesn’t know or care that there is a TCP header in its data; to it, it is just bytes to carry. Think of putting a sealed letter inside an envelope, then that envelope inside a courier bag: each wrapper adds its own label but never opens what is inside.',
         'As a formula: **PDU of layer N-1 = PDU of layer N + control information of layer N-1**. For example, a packet (layer 3) = a segment (layer 4) + an IP header; a frame (layer 2) = a packet + an Ethernet header and trailer.'],
    16: ['**Decapsulation** is encapsulation in reverse, at the receiver. Each layer reads its own header (and trailer at layer 2), acts on it, removes it, and passes the remaining data up. By the time the data reaches the application, every header added by the sender has been removed by its peer.'],
    17: ['Slides 17 to 24 are one animation of a single message going from one host to another. Follow it slowly once: it is encapsulation and decapsulation in action, with real names (TCP, IP, Ethernet).',
         'It starts at the top: an application needs to send data. The application, presentation and session layers prepare it (format it, maybe encrypt or compress it, and keep track of the conversation). In TCP/IP all three are one application layer.'],
    18: ['The **transport layer** (TCP here) adds the **TCP header**. The most important fields in it are the **port numbers**: the source port identifies the sending application, and the destination port identifies which application on the other host should get the data (for example 443 for a secure web server).'],
    19: ['The **network layer** adds the **IP header**, which carries the **source and destination IP addresses**: the address of the sending host and of the final destination host. These two addresses are what routers read to forward the packet across networks.'],
    20: ['The **data link layer** wraps the packet in a **frame**, adding the Ethernet header (with source and destination **MAC addresses**) and a trailer. The destination MAC is the **next device on this link**, not necessarily the final host. Then the **physical layer** turns the frame into bits and sends them as signals on the cable.'],
    21: ['At the receiver, the **physical layer** turns the signals back into bits. The **data link layer** checks the destination MAC address: if it matches (or is a broadcast), it checks the trailer for errors, removes the frame header and trailer, and passes the packet up. If it doesn’t match, the frame is dropped right there.'],
    22: ['The **network layer** reads the IP header. Since the destination IP address is this host’s own, it knows the packet has arrived, removes the IP header, and passes the segment up to TCP (the IP header’s protocol field says it is TCP).'],
    23: ['The **transport layer** reads the TCP header and uses the **destination port number** to decide which application process gets the data. That is how a computer running a browser, an email program and a game at once gives each its own data.'],
    24: ['Finally the application receives exactly the data the sender’s application sent. On the wire, the order of headers from the front is **Ethernet | IP | TCP | data** (and the Ethernet trailer at the end), because the lowest layer’s header is added last, so it is outermost.'],
    25: ['From here the deck goes through each layer’s job, bottom to top. The **physical layer** moves **individual bits** from one node to the next node on the link (one hop). It doesn’t know what the bits mean, which frame they belong to, or where they are finally going; it only makes sure that a 1 sent is received as a 1.'],
    26: ['The physical layer deals with the actual medium. It defines the **physical characteristics** of the interfaces and the media: how devices connect to each other and to the link (topology), the **shape, size and number of pins** of connectors, which **voltages and currents** are used, what **type of medium** (twisted pair, fibre, wireless), and the **transmission mode** (simplex, half- or full-duplex).',
         'It also decides how bits are **represented**: each bit is **encoded** into an electrical or optical signal (for example, one voltage for 1 and another for 0). It sets the **data rate**, the number of bits sent per second, also called the transmission rate. And it handles **synchronisation of bits**: the sender and receiver clocks must agree, so the receiver samples each bit at the right moment and doesn’t miss or double-count bits.',
         'Devices that work only at this layer: the **repeater** (regenerates a weak signal) and the hub. The **network card** also has a physical-layer part (the part that sends and receives signals). They don’t read addresses at all, they only handle signals.'],
    27: ['This is a concrete example of a physical-layer specification: the **RJ-45 connector** used on Ethernet cables. The female socket is in the NIC or networking device, and the male plug is on the cable. Its shape, size and 8 pins are defined by the physical layer, so any Ethernet cable fits any Ethernet port.'],
    28: ['The data link layer’s PDU is the **frame**. Its job is **node-to-node (hop-to-hop)** delivery: moving a frame across one direct link from one node to the next node. It doesn’t plan the whole route; it only gets the frame safely across the current link.',
         'Two duties are on the slide. **Framing**: dividing the stream of bits into frames, so the receiver knows where each unit starts and ends. **Physical addressing**: putting the sender’s and receiver’s physical addresses in the frame header. The **physical address** has many names that all mean the same thing: **MAC address**, hardware address, NIC address, LAN address, local address. It is burned into the network card.',
         'The MAC address says **which device on the current link** should receive the frame. In A ── Router ── B, A’s frame carries the **router’s** MAC as destination, because the router is the next device on A’s link. The router then builds a new frame with B’s MAC for the next link. So the MAC addresses change at every hop.'],
    29: ['Memorise this one-line definition word for word: the data link layer moves **frames** from **one hop (node) to the next**. Compare it with the physical layer (bits, hop to hop) and the network layer (packets, source to destination): the exam often gives you the definition and asks which layer it is.'],
    30: ['The figure shows the frame being built: the data link layer takes the packet from layer 3 and adds a header **H2** in front and a trailer **T2** at the end. It is the only layer that adds a trailer. Between the two data link layers, the logical unit is a frame; between the two physical layers, it is just bits.'],
    31: ['The path from A to F goes through intermediate systems B and E. Each hop (**A to B**, **B to E**, **E to F**) is a **separate** data link delivery, possibly on a different kind of link (Ethernet on one, Wi-Fi or a WAN link on another). The data link layer on each hop knows nothing about the others.'],
    32: ['Station 10 sends to station 87 on a **bus**, which is a shared link. The frame carries **source physical address 10** and **destination physical address 87**. Because it is a bus, every station receives the signal, but each one compares the destination address with its own: only **87** matches, so only 87 keeps the frame, and the others drop it. This is physical addressing at work on one link.'],
    33: ['The network layer’s PDU is the **packet**, also called a **datagram**. Its job is **source-to-destination (host-to-host)** delivery: getting a packet from the original sender all the way to the final destination host, possibly across **many networks**. This is the difference from the data link layer, which only crosses one link.',
         'To do that it needs **logical addressing**: a **globally unique** address that identifies each host on the Internet, called the **IP address**. Unlike the MAC address, which is burned into the card and only matters on the local link, the IP address says where the host is in the network as a whole.',
         '**Routing**: the connecting devices (**routers**) decide the **best path** for each packet towards its destination and forward it. Each router looks at the destination IP address and picks the next hop. That is the core of Chapters 7 and 8.'],
    34: ['The one-line definition to memorise: the network layer delivers **individual packets** from the **source host** to the **destination host**. Note “individual”: each packet is handled on its own, with no idea that it belongs to a larger message. Putting the message back together is the transport layer’s job.'],
    35: ['The network layer adds only a header (**H3**), no trailer. The header carries the source and destination IP addresses. It is added at the source host and removed at the destination host; routers in between read it but keep it.'],
    36: ['This figure joins the last two ideas. The **network layer** sees one **end-to-end** delivery, A to F. Underneath, the **data link layer** makes it happen as a chain of **hop-to-hop** deliveries: A to B, B to E, E to F. One network-layer trip = several data-link trips.'],
    37: ['A packet goes from logical address **A** to **P**, crossing two routers. Look at which addresses change. The **logical (IP) addresses A and P stay the same** the whole way, because they identify the original sender and the final receiver. The **physical (MAC) addresses are replaced on every link**, because each frame only travels one hop: on the first link they are A’s and the first router’s, on the next link the two routers’, and on the last link the second router’s and P’s. This is the classic exam question for this deck.'],
    38: ['The transport layer is responsible for **process-to-process** delivery of the **entire message**. A host runs many programs (processes) at once; the network layer gets data to the right **computer**, and the transport layer gets it to the right **program** on that computer.',
         'The network layer treats every packet **independently**: it doesn’t know that packet 1 and packet 2 belong to the same message, so they may take different routes and arrive out of order. The transport layer makes sure the **whole message arrives intact and in order**: it splits the message into pieces, numbers them, and puts them back together at the receiver.',
         'The transport layer’s PDU is the **segment**. Its address is the **port address** (port number), which identifies a process: for example, port 80 for a web server, 25 for email. So, bottom-up: MAC finds the device on the link, IP finds the host, and the port finds the process.'],
    39: ['The one-line definition: the transport layer delivers a **message** from one **process** to another. If a question says “process to process” or mentions ports, the answer is transport.'],
    40: ['The figure puts the two kinds of delivery side by side. The **network layer** is **host to host**: it gets the data from the source computer to the destination computer. The **transport layer** is **process to process**: a TCP segment from source port **52000** (a browser picked a temporary port) to destination port **443** (the web server’s HTTPS port).'],
    41: ['At the sender, the transport layer **splits** a long message into **segments**, each with its own header **H4** (with the port numbers and a sequence number). At the receiver, it uses the sequence numbers to **reassemble** them in order, even if the network delivered them out of order.'],
    42: ['TCP/IP uses **three levels of address**, one for each of three layers: the **physical (MAC) address** at the data link layer, the **IP (logical) address** at the network layer, and the **port address** at the transport layer. Each answers a different question: which device on this link, which host on the Internet, which process on that host.'],
    43: ['Learn the pairs: **port address with transport**, **IP address with network**, **physical (MAC) address with data link**. The physical layer has no address (it just sends bits), and the application layer uses names such as URLs or email addresses, which DNS turns into IP addresses (Chapter 10).'],
    44: ['The **session layer** **sets up, manages and ends sessions** (connections) between applications, and keeps each application’s data **separate** from the others. Examples on the slide: an FTP program downloading several files at once, several Telnet (remote login) connections from one host, or browsing many web pages at the same time. Each is its own session.',
         '**Dialog control**: the session layer decides who may send when. The two processes can talk in **full-duplex** (both at once) or **half-duplex** (taking turns).',
         '**Synchronisation**: the session layer inserts **checkpoints** into a long transmission. If the connection crashes, the transfer can **continue from the last checkpoint** instead of starting over. For example, if a 1,000-page transfer has a checkpoint every 100 pages and fails at page 523, only pages 501 onward are resent.'],
    45: ['The “syn” marks in the figure are the synchronisation checkpoints from the previous slide. They are inserted into the data stream so that, after a failure, both sides know how far the transfer got.'],
    46: ['The **presentation layer** is about how data **looks** (its syntax), not what it means. Its first job is **translation**: different computers may represent data differently. The sender’s layer converts its format into a **common format**, and the receiver’s layer converts the common format into its own. The slide’s example: typing “Cisco” on a mainframe using the **EBCDIC** code would show up as “¢1½3?” on a PC using **ASCII** unless it is translated. Its other two jobs are **encryption/decryption** (making data unreadable to outsiders and readable again at the receiver) and **compression** (fewer bits, which matters for multimedia such as video and audio).'],
    47: ['The figure shows the presentation layer adding its own header **H6** to the data from the application layer, and the peer presentation layer at the receiver removing it. Inside, the data may have been translated, encrypted or compressed.'],
    48: ['The **application layer** is the one users touch: it provides the **user interface** and the **network services** that let applications use the network. Examples on the slide: a web **browser uses HTTP** to access the World Wide Web, **Telnet** gives remote access to another host, **FTP** handles file transfer, access and management, and **mail services** (SMTP) deliver email.'],
    49: ['The figure shows user applications such as **SMTP** (email), **Telnet** (remote login) and **HTTP** (web) sitting in the application layer. These are the programs whose messages travel down the stack.'],
    50: ['**TCP/IP** was developed in the 1970s. It is a **suite** (a family) of protocols named after its two most important ones, **TCP** (transport) and **IP** (network), but it includes others such as **UDP**. The textbook version has **five layers**. The mapping to OSI: the **bottom four** TCP/IP layers (physical, data link, network, transport) **match** OSI’s bottom four. OSI’s **top three** layers (session, presentation, application) are combined into a **single application layer** in TCP/IP. So the session and presentation jobs (checkpoints, encryption, compression) are done inside applications when they are needed.'],
    51: ['Each TCP/IP layer has its own PDU name: layer 5 application: **message**; layer 4 transport: **segment** (or user datagram for UDP); layer 3 network: **packet** (datagram); layer 2 data link: **frame**; layer 1 physical: **bits**. Exams ask “what is the PDU at layer 3?” or “at which layer is the PDU called a frame?”, so learn it in both directions.'],
    52: ['The same mapping as slide 50, drawn side by side: OSI application, presentation and session become TCP/IP’s application layer, and the bottom four line up one-to-one.'],
    53: ['Know which protocols live in which layer. **Application**: SMTP (email), FTP (file transfer), HTTP (web), DNS (names to IP addresses), SNMP (network management), Telnet (remote login). **Transport**: **TCP**, **UDP** and SCTP. **Network**: **IP**, with its helpers **ICMP** (error messages, used by ping), **IGMP** (multicast groups), **ARP** (IP address to MAC address) and RARP. Below that are the underlying physical networks (Ethernet, Wi-Fi). A common trap is placing ARP or ICMP in the transport layer.'],
    54: ['The **original** TCP/IP model has only **four** layers: **application**, **transport**, **internet** (= network) and **network access** (= data link + physical combined). The textbook’s five-layer version simply splits network access into two. If an exam says TCP/IP has four layers, it means this original model; five layers means the textbook version.'],
}

